($t$Colocasia esculenta$t$, $t$Colocasia esculenta$t$, $t$Araceae$t$, $t$Famiglia botanica: Araceae. Portamento: Perennial. Altezza massima indicativa: 1 m. Believed to be ones of the earliest cultivated root crop, Colocasia esculenta or Taro is a tropical plant characterized by its large fat leaves on the end of upright leaf stalks and its thickened rounded corm. It grows up to 1 m in height and the leaves are up to 50 cm long. The flower is a spathe, yellow in colour, and fused along the stalk. The plant is antibacterial and hypotensive. Leaf decoction promotes menstruation and when combined with other plants, relieves stomach pain and treats cysts. Other plant parts are used in the treatment if conjunctivitis and wounds. The corm is edible when cooked. It can also be dried and grated then made into flour. Young leaves and stems can be cooked as well but need to be thoroughly cooked to destroy the calcium oxalate crystal contents.$t$, $j${"luce": "Tollera: pieno sole, mezz'ombra", "acqua": "Abbondante, terreno costantemente umido", "terreno": "Tollera terreno: sabbioso/leggero, medio, argilloso/pesante"}$j$::jsonb, ARRAY[$t$Scheda in bozza, importata automaticamente da Plants For A Future (pfaf.org): dati non verificati manualmente, da controllare prima di considerarli affidabili$t$, $t$Nome comune (inglese, fonte PFAF): Taro, Elephant Ears Taro, Dasheen, Eddo$t$, $t$Rischi segnalati dalla fonte (testo originale in inglese, non tradotto per non alterarne il significato): The plant contains calcium oxalate crystals. These cause an extremely unpleasant sensation similar to needles being stuck into the mouth and tongue if they are eaten, but they are easily neutralized by thoroughly drying or cooking the plant or by steeping it in water.(Parts of plant are poisonous if ingested)$t$], $j${"irrigazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "concimazione": {"primavera": null, "estate": null, "autunno": null, "inverno": null}, "potatura